From 61d727bb6aa7a5b7148518eaf0accf08a2bdb436 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LegendaryFire <tristan.balon@outlook.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 00:39:04 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Speed up Inkling 2's decode: one launch for a visit's
 projections, split controls

A decode step's small kernels left the cards underfilled (ideas from
peaster/inkling-2-mini-vllm-2x3090's sm86-tune.diff, which credits
snailbrainx/inkling-2-mini-sm80):

- a visit's q, rel, gate, k and v projections (Q8_0) run in one
  launch, k_q8_seg: a warp a row over all five matrices, each with its
  own q8_1 input (k's and v's quantized into their own buffers), up to
  8 rows (verify) reading the weights once. The small ones (k, v: 1024
  rows, rel: 384) no longer run alone: 73.8 -> 62.0 us a visit at one
  row, 2-5 rows 63-76 us against ~92;
- a looped layer's control down projections (4608 -> 64, 3 at once)
  split K eight ways (24 blocks a step were 192), the RMS scales taken
  after (rx * sum(w x) = sum(w rx x)): k_ctl_down 18.9 -> 9.0 us,
  k_ctl_up 9.0 -> 9.5.

Rounding-level changes (the projections within ~1e-6 of the old
kernels, the controls of a double-precision reference).

Two cards, decode without drafting: 1K 48.9 -> 51.6 tok/s, 60K 41.8
-> 44.1, 180K 30.3 -> 31.3; a drafted step's 3-4 rows 8-11% less at 7K.
